{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"jeanlike-hick-azurevase","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\nCalling the long-running certified gate op `review_plan` (and, by the same mechanism, `verify_completion`) through the **rebar MCP server** — the path AGENTS.md *mandates* for tracker/gate ops — returns an opaque transport error (`McpError -32001: Request timed out`) whenever the operation runs long enough to reach its LLM passes. The server keeps running and eventually signs the attestation, but the **client is given no completion signal and no job handle**, so from the caller's side the ticket is left in an *unknown state*.\n\n## Why this is higher-severity than \"observability\"\n\n1. **Unknown ticket/gate state.** The caller cannot tell \"still running\" from \"failed\" from \"succeeded\". The verdict (PASS/BLOCK/attestation) is lost to the caller even though it exists server-side.\n2. **Paid-API waste from re-runs.** The natural agent response to `-32001` is to re-invoke `review_plan`. If the first run is still in flight, the re-invocation starts a SECOND billable multi-call LLM review (Bedrock/Anthropic). Nothing tells the agent the first is still running. This can multiply cost per ticket without bound under retry loops.\n3. **Mandated path.** Agents are directed to route gate ops through MCP; the local-CLI recovery (`review-plan --status`) is a carve-out, not something an MCP-only client (e.g. a hosted agent with no checkout) can reach.\n\n## Reproduce / actual / expected\n\nRepro (observed 2026-08-30, ticket 71b5-1ceb-131a-422d):\n1. `review_plan` over MCP on a plan that passes the deterministic P1–P11 floor (so it proceeds to the LLM four-pass, ~15–20 min per the gate-duration guidance).\n2. Actual: client receives `McpError -32001: Request timed out`. Server completes and signs. `rebar review-plan <id> --status` (local) later shows a signed op-cert (here `foreign_key` — signed by the MCP env `9f1c…`, distinct from the local CLI env `b8a3…`), confirming the review PASSED and no work was lost.\n3. Expected: the caller receives either the verdict, or a durable **job handle / in-progress token** it can poll — and a re-invocation while a review is in flight must NOT start a second billable run.\n\n## Recovery that exists today (documents the \"no data loss\" finding, not a fix)\n\n- Claiming through MCP reuses the same-environment attestation (the signer and the claim run in the MCP env), so the passed review is usable.\n- `review-plan --status` (local, read-only, no LLM) reveals the signed/foreign state.\n- A re-`review_plan` short-circuits ONLY once the attestation is durably visible to that caller; during the in-flight window it does not, which is the re-run hazard above.\n\n## Remediation is a DESIGN decision (do not auto-implement)\n\nOperator direction: pursue **(b) async job-handle over MCP** (return a handle immediately, poll for the verdict; a second call for an in-flight ticket returns the same handle rather than starting a new run) and/or **(c) keepalive/progress streaming** so the transport does not time out and an in-flight run is observable. A design spike (OSS prior art for long-running operations over MCP: progress notifications, streamable-HTTP keepalive, job/poll patterns, idempotency/in-flight de-duplication) will inform the choice before implementation. Idempotency — a second invocation must attach to the in-flight run, never launch a parallel billable one — is a required property of whichever option is chosen.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `review_plan`/`verify_completion` over MCP no longer leaves the caller with an opaque transport timeout and no state: the caller receives either the verdict or a pollable in-progress signal/handle.\n- [ ] A re-invocation while a review for the same ticket+basis is in flight attaches to that run and does NOT start a second billable LLM review (idempotent / in-flight de-duplication), with a test proving no second run is launched.\n- [ ] The chosen contract (poll vs keepalive) is documented for MCP-only clients in docs/mcp-reference.md / docs/plan-review-gate.md.\n- [ ] Portable: the mechanism does not depend on the local checkout being present (an MCP-only client can observe completion).\n\n## Attribution\n\nTo be determined during Phase 1 (this is a filed report, not yet root-caused): the introducing change is whichever work exposed `review_plan`/`verify_completion` as synchronous MCP tools without an in-flight/progress contract. Owner domain: MCP.","id":"d80d-7be7-1c0a-4231","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","plan-review","llm","cost"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"review_plan/verify_completion over MCP time out with no completion signal: unknown ticket state + duplicate paid LLM re-runs"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788133986631408873,"uuid":"750e277d-fb05-42ca-8f69-c29b7556903b"}